{
  "format": "srwb",
  "version": "1.0",
  "notebook": {
    "name": "From Patients to Evidence",
    "cells": [
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "# From Patients to Evidence\n\n**Learning goals**\n1. Simulate a randomized trial and distinguish **true generating probabilities** from **observed sample estimates**.\n2. Compute core effect measures: risk difference, absolute risk reduction (ARR), risk ratio (RR), relative risk reduction (RRR), and number needed to treat (NNT).\n3. Understand **sampling variation**, 95% confidence intervals, and their correct frequentist interpretation.\n4. See why the same **relative** risk can imply very different **absolute** benefits as baseline risk changes.\n5. Separate *statistical compatibility with zero* from *clinical importance* (assumed illustrative threshold = 0.02 absolute reduction).\n\n> ⚠️ All data are **synthetic** and for teaching only. **Not medical advice.**\n\n**Runtime and run order.** Use SciREPL's Python (Pyodide) kernel. Run the code cells in order: `one-trial-simulation` → `coverage-simulation` → `baseline-risk-plot` → `compatibility-analysis`. NumPy is available in SciREPL's Python runtime, and SciREPL provides the `mplot` helper for interactive Plotly charts with browser-rendered labels. This workbook requires no Matplotlib or `micropip` package installation and makes no workbook-initiated downloads or uploads. The `mplot` helper is app-provided, not part of a plain native-Python script.\n\nThis fictional model assumes complete 12-month follow-up and independent binary outcomes within each arm. It is an arithmetic/statistical teaching exercise, not evidence for any real treatment or medical decision.",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "title-goals"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "## 1 · The one trial\n\nFictional trial: **500 patients per arm**, 12-month hospitalization risk\n- Control: true probability **0.12**\n- Treatment: true probability **0.08**\n\nNature \"knows\" these **generating probabilities**. We, the analysts, only see **observed counts** — random draws that will not land exactly on 0.12 and 0.08. The one-trial estimates below are computed from the observed counts. The known generating probabilities are used to check confidence-interval coverage and, separately, to illustrate an assumed constant risk ratio across baseline-risk scenarios.",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "one-trial-context"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "import numpy as np\nfrom math import ceil\n\nSEED = 42\nN = 500          # patients per arm\nP_C, P_T = 0.12, 0.08   # true generating probabilities\nMCID = 0.02      # assumed illustrative minimum clinically important reduction\n\nassert isinstance(N, int) and N > 0\nassert 0 <= P_C <= 1 and 0 <= P_T <= 1\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(SEED)\nev_c = int(rng.binomial(N, P_C))   # observed hospitalizations, control\nev_t = int(rng.binomial(N, P_T))   # observed hospitalizations, treatment\n\n# --- observed estimates (what an analyst actually sees) ---\nr_c, r_t = ev_c / N, ev_t / N\nrisk_difference = r_t - r_c            # treatment minus control (negative = treatment better here)\narr = r_c - r_t                        # absolute risk reduction = -(risk_difference), positive = benefit\nrr  = r_t / r_c if r_c > 0 else np.nan\nrrr = 1 - rr if r_c > 0 else np.nan\nnnt = 1 / arr if arr > 0 else np.inf   # no finite NNT for benefit if ARR <= 0\nnnh = 1 / (-arr) if arr < 0 else np.inf # number needed to harm if ARR < 0\nse  = np.sqrt(r_c * (1 - r_c) / N + r_t * (1 - r_t) / N)  # observed-data SE of the ARR\nci  = (arr - 1.96 * se, arr + 1.96 * se)                  # 95% CI for the ARR\n\nprint(f\"TRUE generating probabilities : control {P_C:.3f}, treatment {P_T:.3f}\")\nprint(f\"Observed events (n={N}/arm)   : control {ev_c}, treatment {ev_t}\")\nprint(\"-\" * 56)\nprint(f\"Observed risk, control        : {r_c:.3f}  (true {P_C:.3f})\")\nprint(f\"Observed risk, treatment      : {r_t:.3f}  (true {P_T:.3f})\")\nprint(f\"Risk difference (T - C)       : {risk_difference:+.3f}  (negative = fewer events on treatment)\")\nprint(f\"Absolute risk reduction (ARR) : {arr:.3f}   (= -(risk difference), positive = benefit)\")\nprint(f\"Risk ratio (RR)               : {rr:.3f}\")\nprint(f\"Relative risk reduction (RRR) : {rrr:.1%}\")\nif np.isfinite(nnt):\n    print(f\"NNT for benefit (unrounded)   : {nnt:.1f}\")\nelse:\n    print(\"NNT for benefit (unrounded)   : no finite NNT for benefit (ARR <= 0)\")\nif arr > 0:\n    print(f\"NNT for benefit (rounded up)  : {ceil(nnt)}\")\nelif arr < 0:\n    print(f\"NNH (unrounded; treatment harm): {nnh:.1f}\")\nelse:\n    print(\"ARR is zero: neither a finite NNT for benefit nor a finite NNH.\")\nprint(f\"SE of the ARR (observed data) : {se:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"95% CI for the ARR            : ({ci[0]:+.3f}, {ci[1]:+.3f})\")",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "one-trial-simulation"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "## 2 · Sampling variation and the 95% confidence interval\n\nOne trial is one draw. Repeating the whole 1000-patient trial many times gives different observed ARRs — that is **sampling variation**, not changing biology.\n\nA **95% confidence interval** comes from a procedure targeting 95% long-run coverage. This workbook uses an **approximate Wald procedure**: actual coverage can differ from 95%, especially with small samples or probabilities near 0 or 1. Any single interval either contains the true ARR or does not. In a real study we generally do not know which; in this simulation the generating probabilities are known, so we can check. The frequentist interpretation is **not** \"95% probability that the true value is in this interval.\"\n\nAcross a finite number of simulated trials, estimated coverage also varies because of Monte Carlo sampling. Compare it with the nominal 95% target rather than expecting exactly 95%.",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "sampling-variation-explanation"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "import numpy as np\n\n# Approximate Wald 95% CI for a risk difference.\n# Reasonable for this example's counts; not an exact coverage guarantee.\ndef one_trial(r):\n    c = r.binomial(N, P_C) / N\n    t = r.binomial(N, P_T) / N\n    d = c - t\n    se_i = np.sqrt(c * (1 - c) / N + t * (1 - t) / N)\n    return d, (d - 1.96 * se_i, d + 1.96 * se_i)\n\nN_SIMS = 2000\nr = np.random.default_rng(7)\nres = [one_trial(r) for _ in range(N_SIMS)]\narrs = np.array([d for d, _ in res])\ncovers = np.array([lo <= P_C - P_T <= hi for _, (lo, hi) in res])\nTRUE_ARR = P_C - P_T\nlo, hi = ci\n\nprint(f\"Our trial (seed {SEED}): observed ARR = {arr:+.3f}, 95% CI = ({lo:+.3f}, {hi:+.3f})\")\nif lo <= TRUE_ARR <= hi:\n    print(f\"True ARR = {TRUE_ARR:.3f} -> our CI contains it\")\nelse:\n    print(f\"True ARR = {TRUE_ARR:.3f} -> our CI does not contain it\")\nprint(f\"Estimated coverage of the nominal 95% CI procedure across {N_SIMS} simulated trials: {covers.mean():.1%}\")\nprint(\"95% is the nominal target, not an exact guarantee for this Wald approximation.\")\nprint(\"Estimated coverage also has Monte Carlo variation across finite simulations.\")\n\n# Match the original 40-bin histogram exactly; do not use Plotly auto-binning.\nhist_counts, hist_edges = np.histogram(arrs, bins=40)\nhist_centers = (hist_edges[:-1] + hist_edges[1:]) / 2\nhist_widths = np.diff(hist_edges)\nmplot(\n    [{\"type\": \"bar\", \"x\": hist_centers, \"y\": hist_counts,\n      \"width\": hist_widths.tolist(), \"marker\": {\"color\": \"#7fb3d5\"},\n      \"showlegend\": False}],\n    title=f\"Sampling variation of the ARR ({N_SIMS} trials)\",\n    xlabel=\"Observed ARR across simulated trials\",\n    ylabel=\"Number of trials\",\n    layout={\n        \"bargap\": 0,\n        \"showlegend\": True,\n        \"legend\": {\"font\": {\"size\": 10}},\n        \"shapes\": [\n            {\"type\": \"line\", \"xref\": \"x\", \"yref\": \"paper\",\n             \"x0\": float(TRUE_ARR), \"x1\": float(TRUE_ARR), \"y0\": 0, \"y1\": 1,\n             \"line\": {\"color\": \"darkgreen\", \"width\": 2},\n             \"showlegend\": True, \"name\": f\"true ARR = {TRUE_ARR:.3f}\"},\n            {\"type\": \"line\", \"xref\": \"x\", \"yref\": \"paper\",\n             \"x0\": 0, \"x1\": 0, \"y0\": 0, \"y1\": 1,\n             \"line\": {\"color\": \"crimson\", \"width\": 2, \"dash\": \"dash\"},\n             \"showlegend\": True, \"name\": \"no effect (0)\"},\n            {\"type\": \"rect\", \"xref\": \"x\", \"yref\": \"paper\",\n             \"x0\": float(lo), \"x1\": float(hi), \"y0\": 0, \"y1\": 1,\n             \"line\": {\"width\": 0}, \"fillcolor\": \"rgba(128,128,128,0.15)\",\n             \"layer\": \"below\", \"showlegend\": True, \"name\": \"our trial's 95% CI\"},\n        ],\n    },\n)",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "coverage-simulation"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "## 3 · Same relative risk, different absolute benefit\n\nThis simplified exercise **assumes a constant generating risk ratio** across populations; real relative effects may differ from one population to another. Under that assumption, the absolute benefit still depends on how common the outcome is at baseline: holding the RR fixed at the known generating value $P_T/P_C = 0.08/0.12 \\approx 0.667$, a high-risk population gains far more in absolute terms — and needs to treat far fewer patients — than a low-risk one.\n\n$$\\text{ARR} = p_C(1 - \\text{RR}), \\qquad \\text{NNT} = 1/\\text{ARR}$$\n\n> **Simplifying teaching assumption:** we treat the generating RR of 0.667 as a fixed, known constant across populations. In reality, a treatment's relative effect may vary with baseline risk and patient characteristics — this section isolates the arithmetic, not an empirical claim.",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "constant-rr-explanation"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "import numpy as np\nimport textwrap\n\ndef _risk_wrap_label(text, width):\n    return \"<br>\".join(textwrap.wrap(str(text), width=width, break_long_words=False, break_on_hyphens=False))\n\n\nrr_known = P_T / P_C                     # fixed known generating RR = 0.08/0.12 ≈ 0.667\nbaselines = np.array([0.02, 0.05, 0.12, 0.20, 0.30])\narrs_b = baselines * (1 - rr_known)\nnnts_b = 1 / arrs_b\n\nprint(f\"Fixed known generating RR = {rr_known:.3f} across all scenarios\")\nprint(f\"{'baseline risk':>13} | {'ARR':>6} | {'NNT':>6}\")\nprint(\"-\" * 33)\nfor b, a, n in zip(baselines, arrs_b, nnts_b):\n    print(f\"{b:>13.2f} | {a:>6.3f} | {n:>6.1f}\")\n\nmplot(\n    [\n        {\"x\": baselines, \"y\": arrs_b, \"mode\": \"lines+markers\",\n         \"line\": {\"color\": \"#2874a6\"}, \"marker\": {\"symbol\": \"circle\"},\n         \"name\": \"ARR = p_C(1−RR)\"},\n        {\"x\": baselines, \"y\": nnts_b, \"mode\": \"lines+markers\", \"yaxis\": \"y2\",\n         \"line\": {\"color\": \"#b03a2e\", \"dash\": \"dash\"},\n         \"marker\": {\"symbol\": \"square\"}, \"name\": \"NNT = 1/ARR\"},\n    ],\n    title=_risk_wrap_label(\"One (assumed constant) RR, many absolute benefits\", 45),\n    xlabel=_risk_wrap_label(\"Baseline 12-month risk in control arm (p_C)\", 48),\n    ylabel=_risk_wrap_label(\"Absolute risk reduction\", 24),\n    layout={\n        \"height\": 540,\n        \"margin\": {\"t\": 100, \"r\": 110, \"b\": 100, \"l\": 90},\n        \"yaxis\": {\"automargin\": True, \"title\": {\"text\": _risk_wrap_label(\"Absolute risk reduction\", 24), \"font\": {\"color\": \"#2874a6\"}}},\n        \"yaxis2\": {\"automargin\": True, \"title\": {\"text\": _risk_wrap_label(\"Number needed to treat\", 24), \"font\": {\"color\": \"#b03a2e\"}},\n                  \"overlaying\": \"y\", \"side\": \"right\"},\n        \"legend\": {\"orientation\": \"v\", \"x\": 0.98, \"y\": 0.98,\n                   \"xanchor\": \"right\", \"yanchor\": \"top\",\n                   \"bgcolor\": \"rgba(22,27,34,0.85)\", \"font\": {\"size\": 10}},\n        \"shapes\": [\n            {\"type\": \"line\", \"xref\": \"paper\", \"yref\": \"y\",\n             \"x0\": 0, \"x1\": 1, \"y0\": float(MCID), \"y1\": float(MCID),\n             \"line\": {\"color\": \"orange\", \"width\": 1.5, \"dash\": \"dash\"},\n             \"showlegend\": True, \"name\": \"assumed MCID 0.02\"},\n        ],\n    },\n)",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "baseline-risk-plot"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "## 4 · Compatible with zero ≠ clinically unimportant\n\nTwo different questions, often confused:\n\n- **Statistical compatibility with zero:** is \"no effect\" among the values reasonably compatible with our data? (Does the 95% CI include 0? What is the p value?)\n- **Clinical relevance:** is the effect large enough to matter? Here we use an **assumed** minimum clinically important difference (MCID) of 0.02 — a value we posit for illustration, not one the data establish.\n\nA p value alone answers neither question well, and crossing an assumed MCID does not settle importance: harms, patient preferences, and costs still matter. Below we compare the CI against **both** the zero line and the MCID line.",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "compatibility-explanation"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "import numpy as np\nfrom math import erfc, sqrt\n\nlo, hi = ci\nprint(f\"Observed ARR = {arr:+.3f}, 95% CI = ({lo:+.3f}, {hi:+.3f})\")\n\nif not np.isfinite(se) or se <= 0:\n    print(\"The normal-approximation test is undefined because the SE is zero.\")\n    print(\"A degenerate Wald interval is not reliable evidence of certainty.\")\n    print(\"Use an interval/test appropriate for sparse or boundary event counts.\")\nelse:\n    z_stat = arr / se\n    p_two = erfc(abs(z_stat) / sqrt(2))  # stable two-sided normal tail probability\n    includes_zero = lo <= 0 <= hi\n    above_mcid = lo > MCID\n\n    print(f\"z = {z_stat:.2f}, two-sided approximate p = {p_two:.3g}\")\n    print(f\"CI includes 0 (compatible with no effect): {includes_zero}\")\n    print(f\"CI lies entirely above the assumed MCID of {MCID:.3f}: {above_mcid}\")\n    print(\"-\" * 60)\n    if includes_zero:\n        print(\"No effect is among the values compatible with this approximate CI.\")\n        print(\"This is not proof of no effect: inspect the full range of the CI.\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Zero is outside this approximate CI under the stated model.\")\n        print(\"The sign and range of the CI describe the compatible effect sizes.\")\n\n    if above_mcid:\n        print(\"The entire approximate CI exceeds the assumed clinical threshold.\")\n    elif hi < MCID:\n        print(\"The entire approximate CI is below the assumed clinical threshold.\")\n    else:\n        print(\"The approximate CI includes the assumed clinical threshold:\")\n        print(\"the data do not clearly resolve that threshold comparison.\")\n\n    print(\"The MCID is assumed for illustration, not established by these data.\")\n    print(\"Clinical importance also depends on harms, patient preferences,\")\n    print(\"and costs, which this simulation does not represent.\")",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "compatibility-analysis"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "### ✅ Checkpoint\n\nTwo new drugs each show a **50% relative risk reduction** in hospitalization.\n\n- Drug A is tested in a population with a **20%** baseline 12-month risk.\n- Drug B is tested in a population with a **2%** baseline risk.\n\nWithout computing anything precisely: for which drug is the absolute benefit larger, roughly how many patients would you treat with Drug B to prevent one hospitalization, and what does this tell you about judging drugs by relative risk alone?\n\n*(Answer in the next cell — try first!)*",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "checkpoint"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "### 🔑 Checkpoint answer\n\n- **Drug A:** ARR = 0.20 × 0.50 = **0.10** → NNT = **10**.\n- **Drug B:** ARR = 0.02 × 0.50 = **0.01** → NNT = **100**.\n\nDrug A's absolute benefit is **ten times larger**, despite identical relative risk. Judging by relative risk alone (\"both cut risk in half!\") hides that Drug B's benefit may be so small that cost, burden, or harm could outweigh it — the same *relative* effect can imply wildly different *absolute* value depending on baseline risk.",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "checkpoint-answer"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "### ✍️ Reader exercise\n\nUsing `one-trial-simulation` (cell 3) as a template, with the same seed (`SEED = 42`), `N = 500`, and `P_C, P_T = 0.12, 0.08`:\n\n1. Change the seed to `2024` and recompute the observed ARR, RR, and NNT. How much do they move?\n2. Halve the sample size to `N = 250` (keeping seed 42). Rerun `one-trial-simulation` first, then `coverage-simulation` (cell 5), `baseline-risk-plot`, and `compatibility-analysis`. What happens to the width of the 95% CI?\n3. Optional: find (by trying a few seeds) whether any seed produces a CI that *misses* the true ARR of 0.040. If coverage is close to the nominal 95% target, roughly how often would you expect that across many seeds?\n\nHints: change `SEED` or `N` in `one-trial-simulation`, then rerun that cell and the later code cells in order. Changing a cell's text does not recompute its saved kernel variables. Other things being equal, the expected CI width scales approximately with $1/\\sqrt{N}$; individual random trials can differ.",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "reader-exercise"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "### ⚠️ Limitations of this workbook\n\n- **Synthetic data.** The trial is a simulation with known probabilities; real trials add messy realities (loss to follow-up, non-adherence, unblinding) not modeled here.\n- **Binary outcome only.** Hospitalization is treated as yes/no within 12 months; no competing risks, censoring, or time-to-event analysis.\n- **Normal-approximation CIs.** This is a nominal 95% Wald interval for the risk difference, not an exact coverage guarantee. Sparse or boundary counts require suitable methods, such as score-based intervals for two independent proportions. A generic bootstrap is not automatically reliable near zero events.\n- **No multiplicity or design issues.** We ignore multiple endpoints, interim analyses, allocation concealment, and pre-registration — all central to real trial credibility.\n- **Frequentist framing only.** No Bayesian posterior or decision-analytic perspective, and \"clinical importance\" is assumed, not derived from patient preferences or cost-effectiveness.\n- **Time- and population-specific NNT.** These are point estimates for this simulated population and a 12-month hospitalization outcome. NNT can differ across follow-up periods and subgroups; this workbook does not provide NNT confidence intervals. If the estimated ARR is negative, the code reports number needed to harm (NNH), not a beneficial NNT.\n\n**Reminder:** synthetic, for teaching only — **not medical advice**.\n\n**Primary statistical references:** [Altman and Andersen, NNT for time-to-event outcomes, BMJ 1999](https://www.bmj.com/content/319/7223/1492); [Wang and Hutson, Smooth bootstrap-based confidence intervals, Journal of Applied Statistics 2013](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4789773/).",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "limitations"
      }
    ]
  }
}
